{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "ShokoohSaremi2004-Cband-cube-PBS-flipflop",
  "Source": "M. Shokooh-Saremi, M. Nourian, M. M. Mirsalehi, S. H. Keshmiri, \"Design of multilayer polarizing beam splitters using genetic algorithm,\" Optics Communications 233, 57-65 (2004). doi:10.1016/j.optcom.2004.01.008 - Table 2, column Flip-flop (p. 63); design settings text p. 62",
  "Notes": "C-band cube polarizing beam splitter, designed by the flip-flop method (FilmWizard) for comparison\nSpecification: Same as GA design; flip-flop settings: initial total thickness 6000 nm, max 20 layers, min layer 10 nm, mesh 10 nm, 25 iterations.\nReported: 19 layers; sum d = 5.96 um; sum(nd) = 9.6674 um; P over band: max 0.9995, min 0.9710, mean 0.9927, std 0.0077; yield 2 %. Fig. 9: Rp ~3 % at 1520 nm; Fig. 8: Rs ~99.65 % at 1520 nm falling to ~98.95 % at 1570 nm.\nNotes: Table order is incident (A) -> substrate (S), kept as is. Thicknesses are multiples of 10 nm (flip-flop mesh). Recomputed sum d and sum nd match the table.\nLayers from Table 2, column Flip-flop (p. 63); design settings text p. 62; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), differs from the paper: ours total 5960.0 nm; P min 0.9710, mean 0.9916; paper total 5960 nm; P min 0.971, mean 0.9927. The mean depends on which 10 wavelengths are taken; the paper does not list them.",
